.TH WEB 1 "September 2026" "webdoc 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
web \- create and open web documents (.web files)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B web
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B web create
.I name url
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B web edit
.I name
.RI [ url ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B web delete
.I name
.br
.B web open
.I name
.br
.B web show
.I name
.br
.B web list
.RI [ directory ]
.br
.B web lock
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B web unlock
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B web set
.I variable value
.br
.B web unset
.I variable
.br
.B web variables
.br
.B web config
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B web
manages
.I .web
files: ordinary files that represent a web URL together with a little
metadata. A
.I .web
file owns all of its own persistent state, so it can be created anywhere,
moved, copied, renamed or deleted with normal filesystem tools. There is no
database of web documents and no mandatory directory for them.
.PP
A reference given without the
.I .web
suffix gets one, so
.B web open docs
and
.B web open docs.web
name the same file and
.I docs.web.web
is never produced. With
.B --path
the argument is used exactly as given.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B create \fIname\fR \fIurl\fR
Creates \fIname\fR.web. Creation never overwrites an existing file; use
.B edit
to change one.
.TP
.B edit \fIname\fR [\fIurl\fR]
Changes the URL, the browser, or the lock state of an existing document.
.TP
.B delete \fIname\fR
Removes the document.
.TP
.B open \fIname\fR
Opens the document in a browser. Refused if the global lock is active or the
document itself is locked. The browser is launched detached: see
.B BROWSER DETACHMENT
below.
.TP
.B show \fIname\fR
Prints one document: its path, format version, URL, browser and lock state.
A directory is refused; use
.B list
instead.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIdirectory\fR]
Lists the
.I .web
files in a directory, defaulting to the current one, in the spirit of
.BR "ls -l" .
Implemented with filesystem calls rather than by invoking
.BR ls (1),
so a file with an adversarial name is only ever a filename.
.TP
.B lock \fR[\fIname\fR]\fB, unlock \fR[\fIname\fR]
With no argument, sets the global lock, which applies to every document.
With a name, sets that document's own lock, which is stored inside the
.I .web
file and therefore travels with copies of it. The two scopes are
independent: a global unlock does not unlock an individually locked
document, and unlocking one document does not disable the global lock.
.TP
.B set \fIvariable\fR \fIvalue\fR\fB, unset \fIvariable\fR
Defines or removes a variable: a name/value pair in the global
configuration. Variables are general, not browser-specific; browser
selection is simply the first thing that uses them. A document created with
.B --browser \fIvariable\fR
refers to the variable by name rather than storing its value, so redefining
the variable affects every document that uses it. A variable used as a
browser must hold an absolute path to an executable, which is checked when a
document is opened.
.TP
.B variables
Prints the defined variables as aligned name and value pairs, one per line.
.TP
.B config
Prints the global configuration state: the version in use, the document
format version, the file extension, the location of the configuration and
state files, whether the global lock is active, and how many variables are
defined. Nothing about individual documents appears, because nothing about
them is stored globally.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --browser " variable"
Open with the browser named by this variable.
.TP
.BI --browser-path " path"
Open with this exact browser executable.
.TP
.B --default-browser
With
.BR edit ,
clears the browser field so the document uses the desktop default again.
.TP
.B --locked
With
.BR create ,
creates the document already locked.
.TP
.BR --lock ", " --unlock
With
.BR edit ,
sets or clears that document's own lock.
.TP
.B --path
Interprets the argument as a literal filesystem path: no
.I .web
suffix is appended and no
.B ~
expansion is performed.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
Reports each step of the operation on standard error. May be given before
the command or after it, so
.B web -v open docs
and
.B web open docs -v
are equivalent. Without it,
.B web
is silent on success, in the usual Unix way.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Prints usage.
.TP
.BR -V ", " --version
Prints the version.
.SH OUTPUT
Results go to standard output; errors and verbose progress reporting go to
standard error. The output of
.BR show ", " list ", " variables " and " config
can therefore be piped into another program whether or not
.B --verbose
is in effect.
.SH BROWSER DETACHMENT
The browser started by
.B open
always runs in its own session with all three of its standard streams
redirected to
.IR /dev/null .
It is reparented to
.BR init (1),
so
.B web
returns immediately rather than waiting for the browser to exit.
.PP
This is unconditional and is not affected by
.BR --verbose .
A browser that kept the caller's terminal would print its own startup
messages into the next shell prompt and could consume the user's keystrokes,
leaving the session apparently hung. Verbose mode changes what
.B web
itself reports, never how the browser is connected. To see a browser's own
diagnostics, run that browser directly.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Operation failed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Refused because a lock is in effect.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/webdoc/config.json
Variable mappings. Defaults to
.IR ~/.config/webdoc/config.json .
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/webdoc/state.json
Global lock state. Defaults to
.IR ~/.local/state/webdoc/state.json .
.PP
Neither file lists individual documents: the filesystem is the source of
truth. Both locations are reported by
.BR "web config" .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
web create postgres https://www.postgresql.org/
web set chrome /usr/bin/google-chrome
web create docs https://docs.example.com/ --browser chrome
web open docs -v
web lock docs
web list ~/Bookmarks
web variables
web config
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xdg-open (1),
.BR ls (1)
